# 4. SMS Permission and Detection

This is one of the most important parts of the application.

The application must be able to detect incoming Telebirr SMS messages from sender ID `127`.

### Permission flow

When the driver first opens the application:

1. Explain why SMS access is needed.
2. Clearly explain that the application only processes relevant Telebirr transaction notifications.
3. Ask for the necessary Android SMS permission.
4. If permission is granted, continue to the main application.
5. If permission is denied, explain which functionality will not work.
6. Allow the driver to retry granting permission later.

Request only permissions that are actually needed.

Use `RECEIVE_SMS` for incoming SMS reception where appropriate.

Use `READ_SMS` only if historical SMS import is implemented and genuinely necessary.

Do not make the application the default SMS application.

Do not collect or upload the driver's entire inbox.

### SMS receiver reliability

Implement a native Android SMS BroadcastReceiver using Kotlin.

Important:

- The receiver must work even when the React Native JavaScript runtime is not currently running.
- Do not depend exclusively on JavaScript event listeners for incoming SMS.
- Process or safely queue incoming messages on the native side.
- Persist received events reliably so they are not lost if the application is closed.
- Avoid processing the same SMS more than once.
- Handle multiple messages arriving close together.
- Recover safely after device restart where appropriate.
- Respect Android background execution and battery restrictions.

The native receiver should forward relevant information to the React Native application when the JavaScript runtime is available.

Do not assume that the application can always display a notification immediately under every Android restriction. Handle notification permissions and failures gracefully.

